#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115078 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "7c480d6a-93a1-56ec-a75c-56402e872c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-4723" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\nR_1 := \\dfrac{1}{2}R_1,\n\\qquad\nR_1 := R_1+(-3)R_2.\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/0a/0a034d98da82572a523d406379ab286d1af6b37820f5c2380822abf611643f83.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "e13311fb-21de-58d4-aed0-18304b48bb0c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4724" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](images/89/89189584fab0cbb79fb0b4ac30a81b41bfd6508de7499c111fd53fb6c0c2c928.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115080 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "ff4b4389-d8ff-5b48-904b-ff405abb4e48" :question/math-academy-id "e-4725" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2 \\,$ and $\\, R_2 :=  R_2+(-1)R_1?$\n\n![](images/e6/e607bba88cc1f15abaa45fc3cf02110243433d60259c56805515ced373054ca0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f42791-6076-59d7-9616-c1c6e6600e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263347} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0171e5-e789-542f-a5f7-eba1924afa10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263921} {:answer/id #uuid "83c09255-43ef-5f68-9b54-ce7b76d1d6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264131} {:answer/id #uuid "f1261446-d7b5-56d0-983f-d6cc1794f353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265118} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a9befa-627b-5dd7-9453-949d4370abbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b0171e5-e789-542f-a5f7-eba1924afa10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263921} :answer-field/id #uuid "0743c94a-34a3-5797-8003-0db16035e4b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4fc937e-2b7d-5514-a470-2dba13ab48f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130868" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f8c9688-c197-506a-a5a0-d3922436be0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2}, R_{2}:=R_{2} + 2R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113353} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d9105e-6220-52d6-8ee5-09ffa8b6be7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2}, R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113354} {:answer/id #uuid "0d22b788-5ce3-5328-b3fb-9961347c46fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2}, R_{2}:=R_{2} - 2R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113355} {:answer/id #uuid "28a00a36-6200-5b02-9757-0d4c8bea8a3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}↔R_{2}, R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113356} {:answer/id #uuid "7787cb76-b821-5198-b8e1-e9c369ecc289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R_{2}↔R_{2}, R_{2}:=R_{2} - \\frac{1}{2}R_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113357}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b8815ffc-8b67-57f0-b4b6-b4aa56c9a27e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1452f7af-82c6-55ad-851f-0af4df86350c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398695} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1a813f-c908-5653-a64d-7211791782ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398696} {:answer/id #uuid "481208ae-3c57-5f00-939f-d1f24b8106ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398697} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ba0c9a-7588-50be-bba0-9e1ec3e66758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398698} {:answer/id #uuid "c0d4923d-606c-58ee-8c6b-a4ff5482ef83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}↔{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ba0c9a-7588-50be-bba0-9e1ec3e66758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398698} :answer-field/id #uuid "d60d7e8d-d42b-5c16-b699-93d0d7f45a76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5202eb69-24ce-5ca6-bebd-52036979fd02" :question/math-academy-id "q-31862" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115250 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a4b135f-df0d-5443-bb93-796f32c380c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Calculated Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b06712de-8fab-5277-8d5f-7d38ad367f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114038} {:answer/id #uuid "b73257d7-c6ca-5949-8e56-2ecc0bcbe2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114039} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e7ec50-a9ba-5bca-85e7-ec4289c1e669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114040} {:answer/id #uuid "1c68484c-d8bb-5cd2-934e-6c8fbe17ddd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114041} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3449e7-1af0-5e04-a8cc-b71f15a7e2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114042}] :answer-field/id #uuid "735d93a8-ec4e-5259-b68b-8462cf155d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114037}] :question/id #uuid "bc5586f3-545c-5b09-9d7f-103310c35e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-42989" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $A^{-1}$.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}A & I\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1} &:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=___________; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & a & a \\\\ 0 & 1 & a & a\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115249 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7f42188-bf51-5546-89fb-e33ba47c4b5e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Row Operations Required To Find the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd916e5-f9d5-53f7-abca-50eaccff2c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327330} {:answer/id #uuid "455613c8-bb09-540c-bb3b-c20aa221192e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327539} {:answer/id #uuid "c3359428-3dc0-5272-aa6c-515479db7b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328640} {:answer/id #uuid "d227293b-cecd-5e05-b7ca-90e76490a0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328780} {:answer/id #uuid "de8333d8-5f11-5e68-a87b-0f815d6b050f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3359428-3dc0-5272-aa6c-515479db7b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328640} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd38e060-b522-5ef6-b8de-d36ed7fe5485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3138d68-b633-567e-bf04-14b148ac2a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43004" :question/problem "The inverse of the matrix $V$ is found using row operations, as shown below. What are the row operations corresponding to each step?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] &  & \\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $V$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $V$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[V\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding row operations are\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2},\\,{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${V}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{V}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
